      "side": 1,
      "message": "Maybe rename this to `freeFlagOnReset()` so the intent is clearer? And we can add a javadoc note cautioning how to not misuse this? (i.e. it must only be called while a generator is being constructed)\n```suggestion\n\t/**\n\t * Arrange for flags to be recycled at the next {@code reset}, without\n\t * recycling them immediately.\n\t * \u003cp\u003e\n\t * Unlike {@link #freeFlag(int)}, this \u003cem\u003ealways\u003c/em\u003e defers: the flags join\n\t * the delayed-free set and are only returned to the free pool by\n\t * {@link #finishDelayedFreeFlags()}, which every {@code reset} invokes. If\n\t * the flags were marked {@code retainOnReset}, that request is cleared.\n\t * \u003cp\u003e\n\t * \u003cb\u003eOnly call this while a {@link Generator} is under construction\u003c/b\u003e —\n\t * i.e. from within the call chain of {@code StartGenerator.next()}. At that\n\t * point {@link #pending} is still the {@code StartGenerator}, so\n\t * {@link #isNotStarted()} reports {@code true} and {@link #freeFlag(int)}\n\t * would return the flags to {@code freeFlags} immediately, while the\n\t * generator being built is about to use them. The next\n\t * {@link #newFlag(String)} would then hand the same bits to an unrelated\n\t * caller, silently corrupting both walks. Calling this from anywhere else is\n\t * almost certainly a mistake; use {@link #disposeFlag(RevFlag)} instead.\n\t *\n\t * @param mask\n\t *            the flag bits to recycle at the next {@code reset}.\n\t */\n\tvoid freeFlagOnReset(int mask) {\n```",
